# Fugue No. 1 in C major, BWV 870 (Well-Tempered Clavier, Book II)
# Three voices, 2/4, 83 bars. Bar numbers match the Mutopia score and
# the MIDI (no pickup: the subject starts after an eighth rest).
#
# Character: bright and energetic. The subject has a short head (G F G C,
# then a mordent on A, falling to G) followed by running sixteenths, and
# that motor rhythm almost never stops. Donald Satz (bach-cantatas.com
# review of WTC II recordings) describes "great energy, speed, forward
# momentum" with tension built and released. It should drive, but at a
# pace where the sixteenths stay clear on the chip.
#
# Tempo: quarter = 84 (eighth = 168), about two minutes. 83 bars of 2/4
# is 166 quarters. Per-fugue timings found: Trevor Pinnock (harpsichord)
# about 2:01, quarter 82; Christopher O'Riley (piano, Navona) 1:55,
# quarter 87; Angela Hewitt (Hyperion, 2008) 1:39, quarter 100. An
# Amazon listing gives Richard Egarr 2:08 (quarter 78) but that listing
# looked unreliable. I could not find per-fugue timings for Gould,
# Schiff, Richter, Gulda, Leonhardt or Gilbert. 84 sits with the two
# harpsichordists, toward the unhurried end of the range.
#
# Structure (worked out from the Mutopia LilyPond voices; the published
# analyses I found give no bar numbers, so this is my own reading):
#   1-4    subject in the alto (mid), C major
#   5-8    answer in the soprano (top), G major
#   9-12   subject in the bass (bottom), C; it runs on in sixteenths
#   13     cadence in C
#   13-19  the head alone, in close imitation one bar apart, rising:
#          soprano 13, 15, 17 and alto 14, 16, 18, over bass sixteenths
#   21-24  subject in the alto, D minor
#   25     cadence in D minor; 25-28 answer form in the soprano, D minor
#   26-38  the bass rests: a two-voice episode, cadence in A minor at
#          29, then sequences in sixteenths with head fragments in the
#          soprano, through F toward C
#   39-42  the bass re-enters with the answer form in C and runs on in a
#          rising sixteenth sequence (to 49); suspensions above (39-46)
#   47-50  subject in the alto, C, over a G pedal in the bass (50-52)
#   51-54  answer in the soprano, G (the exposition's pairing again)
#   55     cadence in G major
#   55-61  the head in close imitation again: soprano 55, 57, 59 and
#          alto 56, 58, 60, rising
#   61-67  falling suspensions in both upper voices
#   68     deceptive cadence onto A minor; head in the bass (68-69) while
#          the alto holds C (68-71)
#   72-75  soprano holds C; the alto has the head low, crossing the bass
#   76-81  tonic pedal: the bass repeats a low C; head in the soprano
#          (76-77); chords from 79 with a flattened A (minor subdominant
#          colour) before the final C major chord at 83

tempo 84               # quarter, see above

dyn 1 mf               # bars 1-12: exposition
lead 5-8 top           # bars 5-8: answer in the soprano
lead 9-12 bottom       # bars 9-12: subject in the bass

dyn 13 f               # bars 13-24: after the C cadence, the head piles up in close imitation
lead 21-24 mid         # bars 21-24: subject in the alto, D minor

dyn 25 mp              # bars 25-38: after the D minor cadence the bass drops out, two voices only
lead 25-28 top         # bars 25-28: answer form in the soprano, D minor

dyn 39 mf              # bars 39-46: the bass returns with the answer form, three voices again
lead 39-42 bottom      # bars 39-42: answer form in the bass

dyn 47 f               # bars 47-67: subject and answer over the G pedal, then the second stretto
lead 47-50 mid         # bars 47-50: subject in the alto over the pedal
lead 51-54 top         # bars 51-54: answer in the soprano

broaden 67 0.94        # bar 67: a little weight into the deceptive cadence
dyn 68 mp              # bars 68-71: deceptive cadence, held C above, quieter
lead 68-69 bottom      # bars 68-69: head in the bass

ramp 72-76 f           # bars 72-76: build onto the tonic pedal (alto head at 72 crosses
                       # the bass, so no lead mark there)
lead 76-77 top         # bars 76-77: last head, in the soprano, over the C pedal

rit 4 0.7              # last 4 short bars: the chords and the final C major chord
